Output: hello goodbye. __getattr__ runs only when normal lookup fails, so p.hi and p.bye forward to the wrapped Greeter.

code:
class Greeter:
    def hi(self): return 'hello'
    def bye(self): return 'goodbye'

class Proxy:
    def __init__(self, obj): self._obj = obj
    def __getattr__(self, name):
        return getattr(self._obj, name)

p = Proxy(Greeter())
print(p.hi(), p.bye())

real example:
Proxy objects forward unknown attributes to a hidden target, like lazy-loaded libraries.

Output: HI hi. Printer stores an injected callable and dispatches to it, achieving behavior variation without subclasses.

code:
class Printer:
    def __init__(self, fmt): self.format = fmt

def upper(s): return s.upper()

def lower(s): return s.lower()

print(Printer(upper).format('hi'))  # HI
print(Printer(lower).format('hi'))  # hi

real example:
Strategy objects passed into a class let one API produce many different behaviors.
